" Obsidian vimrc — loaded by the 'Vimrc Support' community plugin.
"
" What CodeMirror 6's vim does and does not give you here:
"   - `let mapleader` — supported per the README, but every mapping below
"     writes <Space> literally so the file reads like the key you press
"   - `set langmap` (ignored)
"   - `noremap`/`nnoremap`/`xnoremap` family (only `nmap`/`vmap`/`imap`)
"   - <Space> is bound to a motion and must be `unmap`-ed before it can
"     start a chord (done below) — see the plugin README.
" hotkeys.json carries the same actions on the Alt tier, which also works
" in the graph, canvas and settings — places this file never loads into.

nmap <Esc> :nohlsearch<CR>
vmap p     "_dP
" Increment/decrement moved off <C-a>/<C-x> to free <C-a> for select-all.
nmap +     <C-a>
nmap -     <C-x>
nmap <C-a> ggVG
nmap <Up>    <Nop>
nmap <Down>  <Nop>
nmap <Left>  <Nop>
nmap <Right> <Nop>

" ── scheme vocabulary (docs/keybinds.md) ───────────────────────────────
" The Vimrc Support plugin can call app commands: `exmap <name> obcommand
" <id>` then map the alias. Type :obcommand to list ids in the console.
"
" History on the vim jumplist pair, the same keys nvim, zathura and Vimium
" use. Obsidian's own Ctrl+O (quick switcher) and Ctrl+I (italic) were moved
" in hotkeys.json to free them; Alt+[ / Alt+] stay bound at app level for the
" graph and settings, where this file is not running.
exmap back    obcommand app:go-back
exmap forward obcommand app:go-forward
nmap <C-o> :back<CR>
nmap <C-i> :forward<CR>

" H/L are prev/next tab everywhere in the scheme.
exmap tabprev obcommand workspace:previous-tab
exmap tabnext obcommand workspace:next-tab
nmap H :tabprev<CR>
nmap L :tabnext<CR>

" ── Leader tier (docs/keybinds.md §4.1) ────────────────────────────────
" Obsidian is an editor, not a container the way tmux is, so the whole top
" level of the leader tier applies here exactly as it does in nvim — a tab
" is a file (§1). The Alt binds in hotkeys.json stay as the second entrance:
" they keep working in the graph, canvas and settings, where this file does
" not run.
"
" <Space> is a motion (move right) out of the box, and a bound key cannot
" start a chord — every <Space>x below is dead without this unmap. The
" plugin README names this as the required first step.
unmap <Space>
exmap switcher  obcommand switcher:open
exmap gsearch   obcommand global-search:open
exmap palette   obcommand command-palette:open
exmap newfile   obcommand file-explorer:new-file
exmap template  obcommand insert-template
exmap savefile  obcommand editor:save-file
exmap closetab  obcommand workspace:close
exmap sidebar   obcommand app:toggle-left-sidebar
exmap reveal    obcommand file-explorer:reveal-active-file
exmap graph     obcommand graph:open

nmap <Space><Space> :switcher<CR>
nmap <Space>/       :gsearch<CR>
nmap <Space>:       :palette<CR>
nmap <Space><CR>    :newfile<CR>
nmap <Space>w       :savefile<CR>
nmap <Space>q       :closetab<CR>
nmap <Space>n       :sidebar<CR>
nmap <Space>e       :reveal<CR>
nmap <Space>t       :template<CR>
" <Space>d is the dashboard in nvim; the nearest thing Obsidian has is the
" graph — there is no start screen to open.
nmap <Space>d       :graph<CR>

" Tabs are files here (§1), so they sit on the leader digits, same as nvim.
" Obsidian has goto-tab-1..8 and no ninth, so 9 lands on the last tab —
" which is what its own Alt+9 does. 0 is the last tab as in nvim.
exmap tab1 obcommand workspace:goto-tab-1
exmap tab2 obcommand workspace:goto-tab-2
exmap tab3 obcommand workspace:goto-tab-3
exmap tab4 obcommand workspace:goto-tab-4
exmap tab5 obcommand workspace:goto-tab-5
exmap tab6 obcommand workspace:goto-tab-6
exmap tab7 obcommand workspace:goto-tab-7
exmap tab8 obcommand workspace:goto-tab-8
exmap tablast obcommand workspace:goto-last-tab
nmap <Space>1 :tab1<CR>
nmap <Space>2 :tab2<CR>
nmap <Space>3 :tab3<CR>
nmap <Space>4 :tab4<CR>
nmap <Space>5 :tab5<CR>
nmap <Space>6 :tab6<CR>
nmap <Space>7 :tab7<CR>
nmap <Space>8 :tab8<CR>
nmap <Space>9 :tablast<CR>
nmap <Space>0 :tablast<CR>
